## Package comparison

The phase package is aligned with approved Phase 1 and keeps protocol, client, host, and documentation expansion out of scope.

## Findings

### Critical — Provider restart supervision is not implemented

`session/provider.ts:61-75` acquires one `subscribeAttempt` stream, but contains no `Schedule` or reacquisition loop. A terminal callback only queues an invalidation; the callback stream remains open and never starts another provider attempt. The production daemon therefore permanently loses media events after the first stream termination instead of preserving the required 1/2/4/8/8-second restart and reset-after-snapshot behavior.

The new one-attempt seam also violates exact-once cleanup: `system-media.ts:430-439` calls the source disposer on terminal without marking the attempt disposed, so later scope cleanup calls it again, and late `onLine` callbacks can still publish after terminal.

### Critical — Accepted connections are still launched as detached root fibers

`server.ts:252-259` and `server.ts:278-286` invoke `Effect.runFork` from Node callbacks. Supplying a captured `Scope` registers finalizers in that shared server scope; it does not make the root fiber a supervised child. Each connection then waits on `Effect.never`. Natural socket closure only destroys the socket and does not end a per-connection scope, so frame fibers and status/state forwarding subscriptions survive until whole-server shutdown. This directly misses the phase's defining requirement that every accepted socket and connection worker be independently scoped and supervised.

### High — Poll deadlines do not follow authoritative state

`coordinator.ts:272-281` reads state once, delays a poll sample, and repeats with an unrelated one-millisecond `Schedule`. The poll fiber starts before the initial sample, so it can select the initial eight-second idle delay even when initialization immediately accepts playing state. Later snapshots or commands also cannot interrupt and reset an already sleeping deadline. This does not implement the required resettable 3/5/8-second policy.

### High — Interruption and defects are converted into provider failures

Both sampling and transport wrap provider effects in `Effect.exit` (`coordinator.ts:138-143` and `:206-218`) and handle every failure cause as an ordinary provider failure. Scope interruption or a defect during blocked work can therefore degrade status or fail a command as `PROVIDER_FAILURE`, rather than remaining interruption/defect and allowing shutdown finalization to settle callers as disposed.

### High — The claimed verification does not substantiate Phase 1 acceptance

There is no `TestClock` usage in any tasked test. `session-coordinator.test.ts` contains one test, the server suite adds only three basic cases, and `system-media.test.ts` has no coverage of the new one-attempt seam or daemon-owned retry schedule. Missing evidence includes deterministic 3/5/8 polling, reconciliation timing, 1/2/4/8 restart/reset, coalesced/stale sampling, failure continuation, saturation, blocked-work interruption, and exact-once connection/provider cleanup. The coder result acknowledges this residual gap, so passing the existing 33 tests is not sufficient for the package's acceptance checks.

### Medium — Stable command error codes regress at the socket boundary

`server.ts:240-249` maps every failed coordinator submission to `PROVIDER_FAILURE`. Tagged `SERVER_BUSY` and `DISPOSED` failures therefore no longer retain their existing stable boundary codes, contrary to the package's compatibility requirement.
